{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794860992848,"sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","part":{"id":"prt_1e60d4938001UadHHr1Ncdidxt","messageID":"msg_1e60d3d82001wLkSFsjLwXmfIy","sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","snapshot":"58a71bd121f502d659bcf1d61660ec8fc2e94235","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794861013885,"sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","part":{"id":"prt_1e60d6f51001cUo5coBOUGXcXc","messageID":"msg_1e60d3d82001wLkSFsjLwXmfIy","sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","type":"text","text":"Verified everything. Now writing the answers file:","time":{"start":1794861002577,"end":1794861013869}}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794861013896,"sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"call_64fe7524afd14a61b4807401","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/answer-questions/opencode-2/repo/ANSWERS.md","content":"# Answers about the parcels code\n\n## Q1. From what order value is shipping free, and how can that value be changed without editing code?\n\n**Free shipping starts at €60.00** — the order must be *at or above* that value, so €60.00 exactly\nalready ships free (an order of €59.99 does not).\n\n- The threshold is the constant `FREE_SHIPPING_FROM`, defined as a `Decimal` read from the\n  environment variable `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` with `\"60.00\"` as the fallback: `parcels/settings.py:15`.\n- The comparison is `if order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM: return Decimal(\"0.00\")`:\n  `parcels/quote.py:31-32`.\n\n**Changing it without touching code:** set the environment variable `PARCELS_FREE_FROM` to the new\nthreshold in euros, e.g. `PARCELS_FREE_FROM=75.00`. Verified: with that variable set, €74.99 is\ncharged and €75.00 ships free.\n\nThings worth knowing before you change it:\n\n- The value is the order value **after discounts** (`parcels/quote.py:26`) and is in **euros**\n  (`parcels/settings.py:14`).\n- The threshold applies **inside the EU only**. The destination check runs *before* the free\n  shipping check, so a non-EU destination raises `CannotShip` regardless of how large the order is\n  (`parcels/quote.py:29-30`, country list in `parcels/carriers.py:22-24`).\n- The settings are read **once at import** (\"Settings read from the environment once, at import\",\n  `parcels/settings.py:1`), so the process has to be restarted (or the module reloaded) for a new\n  environment value to take effect.\n\n## Q2. What happens to an order that is heavier than a single parcel may be?\n\n**It is split into several parcels — it is not rejected, and there is no maximum total weight.**\nA single parcel may weigh at most `MAX_PARCEL_KG = 31.5` kg (`parcels/settings.py:17`).\n\n- `parcel_weights()` computes `count = max(1, math.ceil(total_kg / MAX_PARCEL_KG))` and splits the\n  order into exactly that many parcels of *equal* weight, each rounded **up** to the gram:\n  `parcels/packing.py:20-22`.\n- The order value is then charged **per parcel**: the shipping cost is the sum of `parcel_price()`\n  over every parcel (`parcels/quote.py:33`).\n- `parcel_price()` is `tariff.base + tariff.per_kg * math.ceil(weight_kg)` plus the EU surcharge\n  for destinations other than NL — i.e. the per-kilogram charge is \"per started kilogram\" of\n  *each* parcel, not of the order: `parcels/quote.py:17-22`.\n\nWorked example (default carrier `postnl`, destination DE, order value below the free-shipping\nthreshold):\n\n| Order weight | Parcels | Weight each | Shipping to DE |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 31.5 kg | 1 | 31.500 kg | €25.75 |\n| 31.6 kg | 2 | 15.800 kg | €37.10 |\n| 70 kg | 3 | 23.334 kg | €66.45 |\n\nThe 70 kg case matches the documented example and the existing test\n(`parcels/packing.py:14-15`, `tests/test_parcels.py:11-12`).\n\nTwo consequences to be aware of when you change anything here:\n\n- Because the parcels are equal in weight and priced per parcel, splitting is not free: the\n  `base` price and the \"started kilogram\" rounding are paid once **per parcel**. Two parcels of\n  15.8 kg cost more than one parcel of 31.6 kg would.\n- The equal split is rounded up to the gram, so the parcels of a heavy order weigh marginally more\n  in total than the order (3 × 23.334 kg = 70.002 kg for a 70 kg order).\n\n## Q3. Which carrier is used when nothing is configured, and how is another one chosen?\n\n**When nothing is configured, `postnl` is used.** `CARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\",\n\"postnl\").lower()` — the environment variable is optional and `postnl` is the default:\n`parcels/settings.py:10`.\n\n**Choosing another carrier:** set the `PARCELS_CARRIER` environment variable to one of the three\nsupported names, e.g. `PARCELS_CARRIER=dhl` or `PARCELS_CARRIER=ups`. The allowed values are\n`(\"dhl\", \"ups\", \"postnl\")` (`parcels/settings.py:8`); the value is lower-cased, so `DHL` also works\n(`parcels/settings.py:10`).\n\nDetails:\n\n- An unsupported value is rejected at import time with `RuntimeError: PARCELS_CARRIER must be one\n  of dhl, ups, postnl, not 'foo'` (`parcels/settings.py:11-12`) — the application does not start.\n- The selected carrier picks the tariff table used for pricing: `tariff = TARIFFS[CARRIER]` in\n  `parcel_price()` (`parcels/quote.py:18`), with the tariffs defined in `parcels/carriers.py:16-20`\n  (`dhl` €4.95 + €0.40/kg + €6.00, `ups` €6.50 + €0.30/kg + €5.00, `postnl` €4.10 + €0.45/kg +\n  €7.25).\n- The choice is process-wide and read once at import (`parcels/settings.py:1`, `parcels/settings.py:10`);\n  there is no per-order or per-country carrier selection anywhere in the code.\n\n## Q4. How is the delivery date worked out for an order placed on a Friday at 17:00? Does it account for weekends and public holidays?\n\nThe calculation has three steps: cutoff, transit lookup, working-day addition.\n\n1. **Cutoff** — `start = ordered.date()`; if `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` the start moves one\n   calendar day forward. `CUTOFF_HOUR = 15` and the comment says \"orders placed at or after 15:00\n   are handled from the next day\": `parcels/delivery.py:11`, `parcels/delivery.py:26-28`.\n   A Friday 17:00 order therefore starts on **Saturday**.\n2. **Transit** — `transit = TRANSIT_DAYS.get(country.upper(), DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS)`, with\n   NL 1, BE 2, DE 2, LU 2 and 4 days for every other country: `parcels/delivery.py:9-10`,\n   `parcels/delivery.py:29`.\n3. **Working days** — `add_working_days(start, HANDLING_DAYS + transit)` with `HANDLING_DAYS = 1`\n   (`parcels/settings.py:18`, `parcels/delivery.py:30`). The loop advances one calendar day at a\n   time and only counts a day when `current.weekday() < 5`, i.e. Monday–Friday:\n   `parcels/delivery.py:18-22`.\n\n**Result for Friday 17:00** (using Friday 2026-03-06, the Friday the existing test week is built\naround):\n\n| Destination | Days counted (handling + transit) | Delivery date | Weekday |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| NL | 1 + 1 = 2 | 2026-03-10 | Tuesday |\n| BE, DE, LU | 1 + 2 = 3 | 2026-03-11 | Wednesday |\n| Any other EU country (e.g. FR) | 1 + 4 = 5 | 2026-03-13 | Friday |\n\n**Weekends: yes.** Saturdays and Sundays are skipped when the days are counted\n(`parcels/delivery.py:20`), which is what the existing test pins down\n(`tests/test_parcels.py:19-21`). Because the cutoff shift to Saturday is itself a weekend day, the\nSaturday is simply never counted: the first counted day is the following Monday. A side effect is\nthat a Friday 17:00 order gets the same delivery date as a Thursday order placed before the cutoff\n(both are Tuesday 2026-03-10 for NL).\n\n**Public holidays: no.** `add_working_days` only looks at the weekday, so a holiday that falls on a\nMonday–Friday is counted as a normal working day and the delivery date can be too early. The\ndocstring states this explicitly: \"Saturdays and Sundays are skipped (holidays are not)\"\n(`parcels/delivery.py:15`). There is no holiday calendar, table, or holiday-related lookup anywhere\nin the repository, so public holidays are not taken into account at all.\n"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/private/tmp/claude-502/-Users-alexander--claude-spares-spare-IwZtqC/50e3bfb1-34ed-4585-ad26-c678e31a5e93/scratchpad/agents-out/opencode-heldout/answer-questions/opencode-2/repo/ANSWERS.md","exists":false,"truncated":false},"title":"ANSWERS.md","time":{"start":1794861013863,"end":1794861013892}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Everything is confirmed. Now let me write ANSWERS.md at the top of the project.\n\nLet me compute the exact numbers for the Q2 example:\n- 70 kg, postnl, DE: 3 parcels × 23.334 kg. Each parcel price = 4.10 + 0.45 × ceil(23.334) = 4.10 + 0.45×24 = 4.10 + 10.80 = 14.90; + 7.25 EU surcharge (not NL) = 22.15; × 3 = 66.45. ✓ matches output.\n\nAlso the \"heavier than a single parcel\" — 31.5 kg max per parcel. 31.6 kg → 2 parcels of 15.8 kg.\n\nAlso note: free shipping check happens BEFORE packing (quote.py:31-33) so heavy orders above threshold still ship free — but the weight is still packed for the quote. Note an important nuance: if order_value >= FREE_SHIPPING_FROM, parcel_weights is never called, so an invalid weight (e.g., 0 or negative) doesn't raise for free orders. That's a side detail; I could mention it briefly since the user asked \"what happens to an order that is heavier than a single parcel may be\" — the answer is split, not rejected. There's no upper limit on total weight (no rejection). Worth mentioning: nothing rejects heavy orders; they are split indefinitely.\n\nAlso note for Q1: the free shipping threshold is \"at or above\" €60.00 — the value is after discounts (quote.py:26) — and free shipping only applies inside the EU (settings.py:14 comment); the country check happens before, so non-EU raises CannotShip regardless of order value (quote.py:29-30).\n\nQ3: Default carrier postnl (settings.py:10). Changed via PARCELS_CARRIER env var; valid values dhl, ups, postnl (settings.py:8); lowercase applied (settings.py:10); invalid → RuntimeError at import (settings.py:11-12). Note it is global for the process, read at import time, and affects the tariff used in parcel_price (quote.py:18).\n\nQ4: Friday 17:00.\n- cutoff 15 → start moves to Saturday (delivery.py:11, 26-28).\n- add_working_days skips Sat/Sun (delivery.py:18-22).\n- NL: HANDLING_DAYS(1) + transit(1) = 2 working days → Tuesday 2026-03-10 (using Friday 2026-03-06).\n- DE/BE/LU: 1+2 = 3 → Wednesday 2026-03-11.\n- Other EU: 1+4 = 5 → Friday 2026-03-13.\n- Holidays: NOT accounted for — add_working_days only checks weekday() < 5 (delivery.py:20), docstring says \"holidays are not\" (delivery.py:15). No holiday table exists in the repo.\n\nOne more nuance worth noting: because the cutoff shifts the start by a calendar day to Saturday, and working days are counted from there, the delivery date for a Friday-17:00 order is the same as one placed on Thursday before 15:00 (both → Tuesday for NL). Wait, the test: Thursday 16:00 → start Friday → 2 working days → Tuesday 03-10. And Friday 17:00 → start Saturday → Tuesday 03-10. Same. Interesting — that's a consequence of the weekend being skipped anyway.\n\nAlso note: the cutoff uses `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — so at exactly 15:00 it's treated as next day. The comment says \"orders placed at or after 15:00 are handled from the next day\" (delivery.py:11).\n\nAlso there's no time-of-day on the delivery date — just a date. And the delivery date doesn't take the order time into account beyond the hour cutoff (minute ignored). Note `ordered.hour >= CUTOFF_HOUR` — 14:59 → same day.\n\nLet me also double-check whether the transit-day lookup uses country.upper() — yes, delivery.py:29 `TRANSIT_DAYS.get(country.upper(), DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS)`.\n\nAlso note the transit day counts are in working days too.\n\nNow write the file. Use citations in the form `path/to/file.py:LINE` or `path/to/file.py:FIRST-LAST`.\n\nLet me get precise line numbers again:\n\nsettings.py:\n- 8: CARRIERS = (\"dhl\", \"ups\", \"postnl\")\n- 10: CARRIER = os.environ.get(\"PARCELS_CARRIER\", \"postnl\").lower()\n- 11-12: validation\n- 14: comment\n- 15: FREE_SHIPPING_FROM\n- 17: MAX_PARCEL_KG\n- 18: HANDLING_DAYS\n\nquote.py:\n- 17-22: parcel_price\n- 25-33: shipping_cost\n- 26: docstring after discounts\n- 28: country = country.upper()\n- 29-30: EU check → CannotShip\n- 31-32: free shipping\n- 33: sum over parcel_weights\n\npacking.py:\n- 11-22: parcel_weights\n- 14-15: docstring\n- 18-19: total_kg <= 0 → ValueError\n- 20: count\n- 21: each quantize\n- 22: return\n\ndelivery.py:\n- 9: TRANSIT_DAYS\n- 10: DEFAULT_TRANSIT_DAYS\n- 11: CUTOFF_HOUR\n- 14-22: add_working_days\n- 15: docstring\n- 18-21: loop\n- 20: weekday < 5\n- 25-30: delivery_date\n- 26: start = ordered.date()\n- 27-28: cutoff\n- 29: transit\n- 30: return\n\ncarriers.py:\n- 16-20: TARIFFS\n- 19: postnl\n- 22-24: EU\n\ntests/test_parcels.py:\n- 11-12: heavy split test\n- 14-17: free shipping test\n- 19-21: weekend test\n\nNow write ANSWERS.md.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_1e60d9a53001JxT1goB3pgXYHM","sessionID":"ses_e19f347b3ffeJ3GudIdYaopciD","messageID":"msg_1e60d3d82001wLkSFsjLwXmfIy"}}
